docs/revision/paper-v2/main.tex:156:\paragraph{Result.} The original claim failed against H2: R3 passed the rule clause, but neither audited baseline met either baseline criterion (Table~\ref{tab:verdict1}). The amended claim passed: under D2+H3 the mean gains were \evone{0.570} for B2, \evone{0.353} for B3, and \evone{0.071} for R3. These are normalised excess focal profits, not losses of honest surplus; and the first report's sentence that R3 matched B3 and B2 under H2 ``within noise'' was unsupported, since no equivalence test was run (published erratum).
docs/revision/paper-v2/main.tex:171:\paragraph{Decision rules.} Table~\ref{tab:rules-v2} summarises them; the protocol file is the authority. Pooled statistics weight the six markets equally, and intervals use a studentized bootstrap (\const{9999} resamples) that resamples seeds within each market, pairing the rules' outcomes on each seed; this measures uncertainty over seeds in the six fixed markets, not over new markets or repeated searches. The gain is $g = (\pi^{A} - \pi^{T})/(S^*/N)$, attacked profit minus the profit of the rule's frozen truthful reference, normalised by the first-best surplus per provider; with $N = \const{10}$ providers, the Q1 margin of $\const{0.05}$ is half a percent of the first-best surplus. \emph{Calibration.} A multiplier $\kappa$ replaces each nominal tail $\alpha_e$ by $\kappa\alpha_e$, so $\kappa < \const{1}$ widens the intervals. It was selected from $\{1, 1/2, 1/4\}$ on fixtures built from frozen development attacks and references on the headline market and dev0 (pools of \const{20000} seeds per market; Q1 in six equal strata), using \const{10000} selection replications, and admitted on \const{20000} independent validation replications if every endpoint's Clopper--Pearson upper bound on its miss rate, at confidence $1 - \const{0.01}/m$ with $m = \const{8}$ endpoints for Q1 and $m = \const{36}$ for Q4 (the two-market branch evaluates \const{24} Q4 endpoints and keeps the denominator \const{36}, which is conservative), was at most its allocated tail. The fixtures hold the policies and references fixed, so admission does not cover search or reference selection, and it does not establish nominal coverage on the fresh, selected-rule distributions. Q1's categories follow the logic of equivalence testing and Q4's market test is an intersection-union test~\cite{berger1996bioequivalence}; the calibration follows the reporting of simulation studies of statistical methods~\cite{morris2019simulation}. Conditional on valid calibrated component $p$-values, Holm controls the Q1 direction family and the six Q4 joint gain-and-efficiency decisions separately; fixture admission does not establish that validity on the fresh-market distributions. Q1's reported intervals use calibrated Bonferroni tails, and its efficiency condition uses a separate nominal one-sided \const{97.5} percent bound. The report stores the calibrated two-sided Q1 value that enters Holm and, for Q4, the raw one-sided bootstrap $p$-values of the two components and the calibrated market value $\max(p'_{\mathrm{gain}}, p'_{\mathrm{eff}})$ with $p' = \min(\const{1}, p/\kappa)$; Tables~\ref{tab:q1} and~\ref{tab:q4} print these with their Holm-adjusted values, and an audit script recomputes these values from the records and reproduces each Q1 category and direction rejection and each Q4 decision.
docs/revision/paper-v2/main.tex:185:\paragraph{Deviations from the protocol.} Two arose during the evaluation. First, the runner recalibrates each fresh market where it runs, and the evaluation ran on Linux x86 machines, while the registered instance file holds the calibration computed on the author's arm64 machine. In each fresh market, between \const{1} and \const{6} provider parameters differ between the two in the last binary digit (one unit in the last place, a relative difference near $10^{\const{-16}}$); the headline market is unaffected. The runner's verification compares configuration hashes exactly and refuses these records. We kept them: a container on the evaluation platform reproduces each run's configuration hash, every differing value is within one unit in the last place of the registered one, and verification passes on every other check when given the reproduced configuration. The check is a script in the artifact. It establishes that the configurations match, not that the decisions are unchanged, since small input differences can change discrete simulation branches; the re-evaluation below holds the selected attacks and references fixed and does not repeat search or reference selection under the registered configurations. We therefore re-evaluated the frozen policies of every fresh-market cell that Q1 and Q4 use on the registration platform with the registered configurations, against the frozen references: per-seed gains change in every cell, cell means move by less than one hundredth, and every Q1 and Q4 decision is unchanged (\texttt{docs/revision/deviation/stability.py}; Table~\ref{tab:ph-stability}). The report's addendum lists the hashes, states the interval labels, and traces search-flag dependencies through each cell's references, which qualifies three further decisions as about the prescribed search procedure. Second, the fresh-market runs cost about ten percent more than estimated, and the author raised the evaluation cap from \const{40} to \const{43} USD and the written authorisation from \const{52} to \const{55} USD so that every registered cell could run; no analysis changed.
docs/revision/paper-v2/main.tex:189:The registered estimates and decisions below come from the report that the tagged code wrote; the addendum beside it (Section~\ref{sec:design}) adds labels and disclosures. Tables~\ref{tab:ph-q1}, \ref{tab:ph-doubled}, \ref{tab:ph-honest}, \ref{tab:ph-q4}, \ref{tab:ph-welfare}, and~\ref{tab:ph-welfare-sens} report exploratory analyses of existing records, and Tables~\ref{tab:ph-stability} and~\ref{tab:ph-q4-stability} the separate registered-configuration stability check; none of these changes a registered estimate or decision. Gains are normalised attacker gains; intervals are those of Table~\ref{tab:rules-v2}. Some descriptive fixed-reference contrasts on fresh1 (\site{r_ext_n} interval entries in the report's per-market Q2 rows) have numerically extreme studentized intervals: the reference-profit contrast there is above rounding scale on only \site{r_ext_seeds} of \site{r_ext_total} seeds (the others are at most \site{r_ext_round} in absolute value), and \site{r_ext_cnt} of the \site{r_ext_tot} frozen resamples (\site{r_ext_share}) omit all three, so their standard error is of rounding size and their bootstrap-t statistics are huge. We retain these frozen outputs, list them in the audit file, and draw no inferential conclusion from them; no other interval in the report has a bound beyond $\const{1000}$ in absolute value.
docs/revision/paper-v2/main.tex:191:\paragraph{Age-ranked versus history-based checking (Q1).} Pooled over the six fresh markets, the prescribed searches leave the identity-replacement attacker a measured gain against C$^*$ higher than against R3 by \site{r_q1_d_est} under D2+H3 and by \site{r_q1_x_est} under X7-SC; the mean gains are \site{r_abs_cs} against C$^*$ and \site{r_abs_r3} against R3 under D2+H3, and \site{r_abs_cs_x} and \site{r_abs_r3_x} under X7-SC. Both direction tests reject $d = \const{0}$ (calibrated input $p' = $ \site{r_q1_d_p}; Holm-adjusted $p = $ \site{r_q1_holm} for each). Both intervals lie inside the registered margin of $\pm\const{0.05}$: the categories are ``\site{r_q1_d_cat}'' (D2+H3) and ``\site{r_q1_x_cat}'' (X7-SC), and the separate efficiency label is ``\site{r_q1_d_eff}''. Under the registered margin, R3 is not materially better than the development-selected comparator; the margin of $\pm\const{0.05}$ is half the first test's comparative margin, not an estimated threshold of buyer indifference, and a narrower margin could order the two rules. The difference changes sign across markets: per market (descriptive), it ranges from \site{r_q1_f3_d} on fresh3 to \site{r_q1_f1_d} on fresh1 under D2+H3. Search uncertainty matters at this level: evaluating the doubled-search winners on the same seeds against the same frozen references (Table~\ref{tab:ph-doubled}, exploratory) changes fresh3's difference from \site{r_dbl_f3} to \site{r_dbl_f3_d}, and the pooled differences to \site{r_dbl_pool_d} under D2+H3 and \site{r_dbl_pool_x} under X7-SC; no decision is recomputed. The two rules differ more in whom they burden than in the attacker's gain. In the registered descriptive honest-replacement diagnostic at the headline market (Table~\ref{tab:ph-honest}), an honest provider replaces its identity at round \const{50}: R3 audits \site{r_hon_r3_0} and \site{r_hon_r3_1} of the replacement's served jobs in its first two ten-round age bins, C$^*$ audits \site{r_hon_cs_0} and \site{r_hon_cs_1}, and B3 about \site{r_hon_b3_0}. Under R3 the replacement earns slightly less per round and receives more refunds on false audit failures, and the operator pays for the extra audits. Both decisions are about the prescribed search procedure: the D2+H3 test uses search-flagged cells, and the X7-SC test uses them through its references (addendum); their nominal error guarantees are not established on the fresh, selected-rule distributions (Section~\ref{sec:design}). The paired descriptive differences in Table~\ref{tab:q1-side} are small for efficiency, audit spending, refunds on false audit failures, and the market service fraction; the reference-profit column is in raw profit units. Splitting $d$ with the same normalisation into the attacked-profit term and the reference-profit term (Table~\ref{tab:ph-q1}, exploratory) shows that almost all of it is attacked profit: the attacker earns more under C$^*$ when it deviates, not when it is truthful.
docs/revision/paper-v2/main.tex:204:\caption{Registered honest-replacement diagnostic at the headline market (report, unrounded): an honest provider replaces its identity at round \const{50} while all others are honest. Audited share of the replacement's served jobs in its first three ten-round age bins; its profit per round and its refunds on false audit failures per ten-round bin, both divided by $S^*/N$ and averaged over the fifteen bins.}\label{tab:ph-honest}
docs/revision/paper-v2/main.tex:223:\paragraph{Repeating the comparison on six new markets (Q4).} The registered B3--R3 gain-and-efficiency comparison under D2+H3 passes on \site{r_q4_k} of the six markets (Table~\ref{tab:q4}), and all three passes (fresh1, fresh3, fresh4) are about the prescribed search procedure. On every market the estimated B3 minus R3 gain difference is positive and R3 keeps its efficiency condition; where the comparison fails, the difference is smaller than the registered threshold, not reversed: \site{r_q4_f0} on fresh0, \site{r_q4_f2} on fresh2, and \site{r_q4_f5} on fresh5, against \site{r_q4_f1} on fresh1. R3's efficiency on fresh2, the lowest, is \site{r_q4_f2_eff}. The decisions on fresh0, fresh1, fresh3, and fresh4 are about the prescribed search procedure (fresh0 and fresh3 through their references; addendum). The registered prediction of at least five passing markets failed. Q4 does not test the complete amended claim of the first test. Table~\ref{tab:ph-q4} (exploratory) decomposes each market's gain difference, which is mostly attacked profit. R3's reported audit spending is about five percent of the realised first-best surplus, and the market's service fraction (all jobs served over the first-best number) is near one. The three non-passing markets have smaller attacked-profit reductions; these descriptive diagnostics do not identify their cause.
docs/revision/paper-v2/main.tex:240:\paragraph{Which parts of R3 matter (Q2).} Under separately searched D2+H3 attacks, pooled over the fresh markets, the main effects on the gain are \site{r_q2_p} for the priority phase P and \site{r_q2_g} for newest-first residual targeting G, both reductions, and \site{r_q2_s} for pacing S, a small increase (X7-SC: \site{r_q2_s_x}). The two targeting parts are substitutes: the PG factorial contrast, half the difference between G's conditional effects, is \site{r_q2_pg}; G changes the gain by \site{r_q2_g_p0} without the priority phase and by only \site{r_q2_g_p1} with it. In the pooled contrasts, either P or G alone accounts for most of the measured reduction; adding the other contributes little. The pattern also appears in the fixed-attack contrasts averaged over the eight selected attacks (P: \site{r_q2_fixed_p}). Our registered conjectures (P negligible, the targeting parts additive, S helpful) failed as scored (Table~\ref{tab:predictions}); the stricter eligibility reading of Table~\ref{tab:pred-eligibility} leaves them not assessed, with the estimates unchanged. X7-SC agrees (Table~\ref{tab:q2-x7sc}).
